{"kind":"assert","text":"The spike's intermittent failure is TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects. dcc02574 said the underlying intermittent was not yet diagnosed and that capturing the hidden stderr was the obvious next step; I did that, and the stderr turned out to be the wrong place to look.\n\nThe capture. I looped spike/cmd/gitseq-report until it failed, and read the written evidence from a failing run. Every one of the six cases carried `pass`, overall status was `fail`, and the \"Test runner stderr\" section was empty. So the diagnosis dcc02574 expected to find in stderr was never there: `go test -json` puts failure detail on stdout, which the reporter parses and then discards for anything outside its case map.\n\nThe actual failure. The reporter runs `go test -count=1 -json ./...` from the module root, so it runs the whole repository rather than the spike packages. Running that directly reproduced it: `--- FAIL: TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects (5.20s)`, at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go:1337, with `elapsed=3.821141541s` and `degraded:true` in the result. Every other package passed, with internal/kernel at 207s and cmd/gs at 193s in the same run — the load that pushes the bound over.\n\nSo the two open requests are one causal chain rather than two independent problems. The test in f6383a92 is the thing that fails; the reporting in dcc02574 is why nobody could see it. That matters for sequencing: fixing the reporter alone would leave CI red, and fixing the test alone would leave the next unrelated failure just as invisible.\n\nNote also that the failing test belongs to no case in the reporter's map, which is why the published table can show six passing cases above a failing total. That is a sharper statement of dcc02574's point than \"the harness died\": the harness ran fine and reported honestly on everything it was told to watch, while the thing that actually failed was outside its field of view.\n\nCodex has since raised the ceiling to `orientationTimeout + 2*time.Second` on request/anti-rollback-posture. That clears my observed 3.821s by 179 milliseconds, which is better than the two-millisecond margin f6383a92 measured but still thin for a machine running two agents' gates concurrently."}